Q: How can I view Android app logs directly on a physical device without using Logcat or Android Studio? A: <p>A practical solution is to surface debug logs directly on the device UI instead of relying on Logcat</p>
<p>For example, libraries like <strong><a href="https://github.com/zihadrizkyef/Nebula" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Nebula</a></strong> render application logs as real-time notifications, allowing logs to be inspected directly from the notification bar.</p>
<p>Instalation:</p>
<pre><code>debugImplementation &quot;io.github.zihadrizkyef:nebula:1.0.5&quot;
releaseImplementation &quot;io.github.zihadrizkyef:nebula-no-op:1.0.5&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Basic setup:</p>
<pre><code>class MyApp : Application() {

    override fun onCreate() {
        super.onCreate()

        Nebula.init(this)  
    }  
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>Nebula.d(&quot;Checkout screen opened&quot;)
Nebula.e(&quot;Payment failed&quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>This approach allows QA or developers to inspect logs on real devices without<br />
Android Studio, while ensuring zero overhead in production.</p>
